    <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"http://schema.org","@type":"QAPage","name":"Python Continue-as-new and signals draining","datePublished":"2025-06-14T21:58:34.329Z","mainEntity":{"@type":"Question","answerCount":7,"author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Yaroslav_Mytso","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/Yaroslav_Mytso"},"dateModified":"2025-06-14T21:58:34.410Z","datePublished":"2025-06-14T21:58:34.329Z","name":"Python Continue-as-new and signals draining","text":"Hey there,\n\nI’m currently implementing workflow which will act like a queue. It will receive tasks via signals and then run child workflows. To omit long history I’m using continue-as-new temporal feature.\n\nNow  I investigate how signals are passed between current run and next one spawned by continu&hellip;","upvoteCount":0,"acceptedAnswer":[{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Yaroslav_Mytso","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/Yaroslav_Mytso"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T13:04:19.678Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T13:04:19.658Z","text":"Actually my further investigation shows that continue_as_new method just finishes with error if some other signal were received between sleep and continue_as_new, then workflow server schedules another WFT to worker which will now catch new signal. But maybe my considerations are wrong.\n\n<a class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex016/uploads/temporal/original/2X/4/4b4b99972462dd7c0970d035e0a7c4007f988da8.png\" data-download-href=\"/uploads/short-url/aK5MvLftjtqFDhjhWEgszg4uC48.png?dl=1\" title=\"image\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">[image]<\/a>","upvoteCount":2,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/6"}],"suggestedAnswer":[{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"awwx","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/awwx"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T07:46:14.990Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T07:41:06.371Z","text":"You’re not going to miss signals scoped to the workflow id.  They’ll either be delivered to the current workflow or to the continue-as-new workflow.\n\nBut, if you start doing some work in response to a signal (i.e., there’s something you’ve called await on), you’ll want to finish that work before cal&hellip;","upvoteCount":0,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/2"},{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Yaroslav_Mytso","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/Yaroslav_Mytso"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T10:50:19.263Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T10:50:19.248Z","text":"I understand that workflows executions are single threaded, but temporal server is not. Signals do not sends directly to worker, but to temporal server and then temporal server schedules a task to workflow worker.\n\n“pause a workflow” is to give possibility to Signal 2 to be stored on TemporalServer &hellip;","upvoteCount":0,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/3"},{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"awwx","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/awwx"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T11:06:16.650Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T11:06:16.638Z","text":"I’m not clear on what your conclusion is.  Your concern is that signal N2 would be lost in this scenario?","upvoteCount":0,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/4"},{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"awwx","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/awwx"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T11:47:06.089Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T11:47:06.075Z","text":"If you workflow worker crashed during processing in one of your signal handlers, the signal wouldn’t be lost because the Temporal Service atomically saves the workflow step and the signal having been delivered together.  When the workflow is replayed the signal is still available to be delivered bec&hellip;","upvoteCount":0,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/5"},{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Yaroslav_Mytso","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/Yaroslav_Mytso"},"dateModified":"2025-06-15T13:35:39.876Z","datePublished":"2025-06-15T13:35:39.858Z","text":"PS. Yep, it looks like it’s true, there’s a special error code that is returned when workflow tries to finish with unhandled signals. <a href=\"https://docs.temporal.io/references/errors#unhandled-command\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">Errors | Temporal Platform Documentation<\/a>","upvoteCount":1,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/7"},{"@type":"Answer","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"awwx","url":"https://community.temporal.io/u/awwx"},"dateModified":"2025-06-16T01:18:01.497Z","datePublished":"2025-06-16T01:18:01.486Z","text":"Aha, thank you for figuring that out.","upvoteCount":0,"url":"https://community.temporal.io/t/python-continue-as-new-and-signals-draining/17682/8"}]}}</script>
  </head>
